issues.problematic_code_snippet:
class WebServer: WebServerProtocol, @unchecked Sendable {
    static let sharedInstance = WebServer()

    private let logger: Logger

    let server = GCDWebServer()

func handleServerTrust(
    challenge: URLAuthenticationChallenge,
    dispatchQueue: DispatchQueueInterface,
    completionHandler: @escaping @MainActor (URLSession.AuthChallengeDisposition, URLCredential?) -> Void
) {
    dispatchQueue.async {
        let origin = "\(challenge.protectionSpace.host):\(challenge.protectionSpace.port)"

        guard let trust = challenge.protectionSpace.serverTrust,
              let cert = SecTrustCopyCertificateChain(trust) as? [SecCertificate],
              self.profile.certStore.containsCertificate(cert[0], forOrigin: origin)
        else {
            completionHandler(.performDefaultHandling, nil)
            return
        }

public func ensureMainThread<T>(execute work: @escaping @MainActor () -> T) -> T? {
    if Thread.isMainThread {
        return MainActor.assumeIsolated {
            return work()
        }
    } else {
        DispatchQueue.main.async {
            _ = work()
        }
        return nil
    }
}

func throttle(completion: @escaping @MainActor () -> Void) {
    lastExecutionTime = Date()
    guard threshold <= 0 || lastExecutionTime.timeIntervalSinceNow < -threshold else { return }
    DispatchQueue.main.async(execute: completion)
}

findings.body:
The PR moves `lastExecutionTime = Date()` above the guard:

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Utils/MainThreadThrottler.swift" lines="25-30"}

Right after the assignment, `lastExecutionTime.timeIntervalSinceNow` is about `-0.000001`. That is never `< -threshold` for any positive threshold, so the guard always returns early and `completion` is **never dispatched**.

The only case that still works is `threshold <= 0`.

::callout{type="warning"}
Every production user passes a positive delay: `HomepageViewController` (0.5s), `StoriesFeedViewController` (impressions and scroll, 0.2s each) and `WindowSimpleTabsCoordinator`. Their throttled work, including homepage impression telemetry, will silently stop. Two existing tests in `MainThreadThrottlerTests` (`testMultipleFastConsecutiveCallsAreThrottledAndExecutedAtMostOneTime`, `testSecondCallAfterDelayThresholdCallsBothClosures`) will fail.

This change also has nothing to do with removing `@Sendable`. Restore the original order:

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/Client/Utils/MainThreadThrottler.swift" lines="26-29"}
    func throttle(completion: @escaping @MainActor () -> Void) {
        guard threshold <= 0 || lastExecutionTime.timeIntervalSinceNow < -threshold else { return }
        lastExecutionTime = Date()
        DispatchQueue.main.async(execute: completion)
    }

`handleServerTrust` runs its body on `dispatchQueue`, which is `userInitiatedQueue`, which in production is `DispatchQueue.global(qos: .userInitiated)`. The PR removed the `ensureMainThread` wrapper on the default-handling path:

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/BrowserViewController/Extensions/BrowserViewController+WebViewDelegates.swift" lines="1334-1351"}

With this change, `completionHandler`, which is typed `@MainActor`, is called synchronously from a nonisolated background closure. There are two possible outcomes:
- Under strict Swift 6 checking, this should be rejected: you can't call a main actor-isolated function synchronously from a nonisolated context.
- If it compiles because of looser checking or `@preconcurrency` inference, WebKit's auth-challenge completion runs off the main thread. That breaks the isolation contract and risks main-thread-checker crashes or UB.

The `.useCredential` path just below still hops to main, so the two exits now behave differently. Removing `@Sendable` doesn't require this change; put the hop back:

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/BrowserViewController/Extensions/BrowserViewController+WebViewDelegates.swift" lines="1342-1344"}
            else {
                ensureMainThread {
                    completionHandler(.performDefaultHandling, nil)
                }
                return

The generic overload now returns `T?`:

::diff{file="BrowserKit/Sources/Common/Utilities/DispatchQueueHelper.swift" lines="22-33"}

This is an unrelated change to a public BrowserKit API, and the semantics are confusing:
- On the main thread it returns the value.
- Off the main thread it returns `nil` even though the work still runs later.

A caller can't distinguish "the work returned nil" from "the work was deferred". That invites code that treats `nil` as a result.

No caller in the repo uses the return value (no `= ensureMainThread` / `return ensureMainThread` matches). The function isn't marked `@discardableResult` either. Any caller that resolves to this overload and ignores the result will now trigger an unused-result warning. Revert to returning `Void` and only drop `@Sendable`:

::suggestion{file="BrowserKit/Sources/Common/Utilities/DispatchQueueHelper.swift" lines="22-33"}
public func ensureMainThread<T>(execute work: @escaping @MainActor () -> T) {
    if Thread.isMainThread {
        MainActor.assumeIsolated {
            _ = work()
        }
    } else {
        DispatchQueue.main.async {
            _ = work()
        }
    }
}

`WebServer` was `final class` and is now `class`:

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Application/WebServer.swift" lines="16-19"}

The class is `@unchecked Sendable` and has a FIXME about thread safety. Leaving it open to subclassing weakens the guarantee further, because a subclass could add mutable state that the unchecked conformance silently covers. It also disables static dispatch. Nothing in the repo subclasses it, and the change has nothing to do with `@Sendable` removal. Restore `final`.

Removing `@Sendable` left two spaces behind:

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/RelayController.swift" lines="10-10"}

